# Security Policy

HDEX Q1 is built with security as a core product guarantee—not as an optional feature.

The runtime is designed to provide secure, deterministic, offline-capable execution for sensitive workloads while enforcing strict policy controls and generating verifiable audit evidence.

---

# Supported Versions

| Version | Support Status |
|----------|----------------|
| `main` | ✅ Active development & security updates |
| Latest pre-release | ✅ Supported |
| Older commits & experimental branches | ❌ Not supported |

---

# Security Principles

HDEX Q1 is built around four primary guarantees:

- 🔐 Secure execution
- ♻️ Deterministic execution
- 📦 Offline operation
- 📋 Auditable execution

The runtime follows a **fail-closed** security model.

If HDEX Q1 cannot verify that an operation is safe, authorized, and policy-compliant, it will deny the operation.

---

# Core Security Invariants

The following guarantees must never be weakened.

- Jobs are untrusted by default.
- Workers are treated as untrusted until verified.
- Filesystem access is denied unless explicitly allowed.
- Network access is denied unless explicitly allowed.
- Policies fail closed.
- Shell execution is disabled by default.
- Local APIs always require authentication.
- Secrets are never written to logs.
- Audit logs must remain tamper-evident.
- Suspicious outputs are quarantined.
- Resource limits are enforced before execution.
- Offline mode must never contact external services.

---

# Threat Model

HDEX Q1 is designed to defend against:

| Threat | Protection |
|---------|------------|
| Unauthorized filesystem access | Explicit mount restrictions |
| Sandbox escape | Process, filesystem and resource isolation |
| Malicious workers | Worker verification and policy enforcement |
| Policy tampering | Signature verification |
| Job tampering | Job hashing and signature validation |
| Dependency poisoning | Lockfile and integrity verification |
| Secret leakage | Environment scrubbing and redaction |
| Command injection | No-shell execution |
| Resource exhaustion | CPU, memory and process limits |
| Audit tampering | Tamper-evident audit chain |
| Offline bypass | Offline verification tests |

---

# Security Architecture

The trusted runtime is intentionally small.

```
            HDEX Q1 Runtime
                   │
      ┌────────────┼────────────┐
      │            │            │
 Policy Engine  Secure Sandbox  Audit Layer
      │            │            │
      └────────────┼────────────┘
                   │
           Worker Execution
```

---

# Trust Boundaries

| Component | Trust Level |
|-----------|-------------|
| Runtime Core | Trusted |
| Policy Engine | Trusted |
| Sandbox | Trusted |
| Audit System | Trusted |
| Submitted Jobs | Untrusted |
| Workers | Semi-trusted |
| Worker Inputs | Untrusted |
| Worker Outputs | Untrusted until verified |
| Local API Clients | Untrusted until authenticated |
| Artifacts | Untrusted until verified |

